## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Probability)
## DBchapter(Continuous distributions)
## DBsection(Application of a normal distribution)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## MLT(Zcompare)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('3')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'normal distribution', 'z score')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',   'PGML.pl',
    'randomPerson.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

@subjects = (
    'Calculus', 'Biology',    'Spanish', 'History',
    'Physics',  'Statistics', 'English', 'Chemistry',
    'Communications'
);
@order = random_subset(4, 0 .. $#subjects);

@mean = (
    random(65, 70),
    random(72, 75),
    random(80, 83),
    random(78, 83),
    random(55, 70),
    random(60, 72),
    random(50, 65),
    random(65, 80)
);
@dev   = map { random(8, 16, 2) } (0 .. $#mean);
@z     = random_subset(4, map { ($_ - 4) * 0.25 } (0 .. 12));
@grade = map { $mean[$_] + $dev[$_] * $z[$_] } (0 .. 3);

$maxi = 0;
$maxz = $z[0];
for (1 .. 3) {
    if ($z[$_] > $maxz) {
        $maxi = $_;
        $maxz = $z[$_];
    }
}

$p  = randomPerson();
$rb = RadioButtons([ @subjects[@order] ], $maxi, labels => 'ABC');

BEGIN_PGML
[$p] took 4 courses last semester: [$subjects[$order[0]]], [$subjects[$order[1]]],
[$subjects[$order[2]]],[$subjects[$order[3]]].
The means and standard deviations for the final exams, and [$p]'s scores are given 
in the table below. Convert [$p]'s scores into z-scores. 

[#
    [.Subject.][.Mean.][.Std. Dev.] [. Score.] [.z-score.]*{headerrow => 1}
    [. [$subjects[$order[0]]] .] [. [$mean[0]] .][.[$dev[0]].] [. [$grade[0]] .] [.[_]{$z[0]} .]*
    [. [$subjects[$order[1]]] .] [. [$mean[1]] .][.[$dev[1]].] [. [$grade[1]] .][.[_]{$z[1]} .]*
    [. [$subjects[$order[2]]] .] [. [$mean[2]] .][.[$dev[2]].] [. [$grade[2]] .][.[_]{$z[2]} .]*
    [. [$subjects[$order[3]]] .] [. [$mean[3]] .][.[$dev[3]].] [. [$grade[3]] .][.[_]{$z[3]} .]*
#]{padding => 0.25}

On what exam did [$p] have the highest relative score? 

    [_]{$rb}
END_PGML

$maxsubj = $subjects[ $order[$maxi] ];

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall that a [`z`]-score for a give data value [`x`] that comes from a normal distribution with mean [`\mu`] and standard deviation [`\sigma`] is 

[``` z = \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}```]

If we use this to calculate the [`z`] score for each test: 

- [$subjects[$order[0]]]: [`` z = \frac{[$grade[0]]-[$mean[0]]}{[$dev[0]]} = [$z[0]] ``]

- [$subjects[$order[1]]]: [`` z = \frac{[$grade[1]]-[$mean[1]]}{[$dev[1]]} = [$z[1]] ``]

- [$subjects[$order[2]]]: [`` z = \frac{[$grade[2]]-[$mean[2]]}{[$dev[2]]} = [$z[2]] ``]

- [$subjects[$order[0]]]: [`` z = \frac{[$grade[3]]-[$mean[3]]}{[$dev[3]]} = [$z[3]] ``]

And since the largest [`z`]-score is [$maxz], this occurs in [$maxsubj] and the answer is *[$rb]****.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
